# Praxis C++ Style & Programming Guidelines

## General Principles
- Use latest stable C++ standard (currently C++23) where compiler support is available; fallback to C++20 minimum.
- Use English for all code and documentation.
- Always declare the type of each variable and function (parameters and return value).
- Prefer immutability for data; use `const` and `constexpr` where possible.
- Avoid magic numbers; define constants.
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions and comments.
- Require Doxygen-style documentation for all public APIs, non-obvious code, new features, and infrastructure changes.
- Require architectural documentation for new modules and major infrastructure changes.
- Keep documentation up to date with code changes.
- Follow the one-definition rule (ODR).

## Naming & File Conventions
- **Files**: snake_case for `.cpp`, PascalCase for headers.
- **Directories**: snake_case.
- **Classes/Structs**: PascalCase.
- **Methods/Functions/Variables**: camelCase.
- **Member Variables**: camelCase with `m_` prefix.
- **Constants/Enums/Macros**: ALL_CAPS with underscores.
- **Namespaces**: lower case.
- **Environment Variables**: UPPERCASE.
- Start each function name with a verb.
- Use verbs for boolean variables (e.g., `isLoading`, `hasError`).
- Use complete words, not abbreviations, except for standard or well-known cases (API, URL, i, j, k, err, ctx, req, res).
- Sort and organize imports/includes.
- Sort using declarations.
- Fix namespace comments.

## Formatting & Layout
- Indent with 2 spaces, never tabs.
- Column limit: 100 characters.
- Namespace indentation: Inner.
- Access modifier offset: -2.
- Attach braces to control statements and function/class definitions.
- Allow short functions on a single line, but not if/loops.
- Always break template declarations.
- Pointer and reference alignment: left.
- Avoid blank lines within a function.

## Functions
- Write short, single-purpose functions (<20 instructions).
- Name functions with a verb and a noun.
- Boolean-returning: use `isX`, `hasX`, `canX`.
- Void-returning: use `executeX`, `saveX`, etc.
- Avoid nesting by early returns and utility extraction.
- Use standard library algorithms to avoid deep nesting.
- Use lambdas for simple operations; named functions for complex ones.
- Use default parameter values instead of null checks.
- Reduce parameter count using structs/classes for grouping.
- Use a single level of abstraction per function.

## Classes & Data
- Follow SOLID principles.
- Prefer composition over inheritance.
- Declare interfaces as abstract classes or concepts.
- Write small, single-purpose classes (<200 instructions, <10 public methods/properties).
- Use the Rule of Five (or Rule of Zero) for resource management.
- Make member variables private; provide getters/setters as needed.
- Use const-correctness for member functions.
- Encapsulate data in composite types; avoid primitive obsession.
- Use internal validation in classes, not in functions.
- Use `std::optional` for nullable values.

## Memory Management
- Prefer smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) over raw pointers.
- Use RAII for resource management.
- Avoid memory leaks; use standard containers (`std::vector`, etc.) over C-style arrays.

## Exceptions & Error Handling
- Use exceptions for unexpected errors only.
- Catch exceptions to fix, add context, or escalate; otherwise, use a global handler.
- Use `std::optional`, `std::expected`, or error codes for expected failures.

## Project Structure
- Use modular architecture; organize code into modules: core, graphics, audio, physics, input, scene, utils.
- Mirror `include/` and `src/` structure.
- Place tests in `tests/`, mirroring the module structure.
- Use latest stable CMake version (3.20+ minimum) as the build system; prefer Ninja as the generator.
- Support multi-platform builds (Windows, Linux, macOS).
- Use CMake FetchContent or Git submodules for dependencies.
- Organize build targets modularly for selective feature inclusion.
- Provide separate build configs: Debug, Release, RelWithDebInfo.
- Integrate unit tests with CTest.
- Enable latest versions of Clang-Tidy and Clang-Format for all code.
- Check for null dereferences and memory leaks in CI/builds.
- No proprietary or costly tools; only open, proven libraries (see README).
- Always use latest stable versions of dependencies when possible and sensible:
  - SDL 3.x (latest stable)
  - Vulkan SDK (latest LTS)
  - GLM (latest stable)
  - spdlog (latest stable)
  - fmt (latest stable)
  - EnTT (latest stable)
  - Catch2 (latest stable)
  - Other dependencies should follow the same principle

## Standard Library & Modern C++
- Use the latest C++ Standard Library features whenever possible.
- Prefer `std::string` over C-style strings.
- Use `std::vector`, `std::map`, `std::unordered_map`, etc. for collections.
- Use `std::optional`, `std::variant`, `std::any` for type safety.
- Use `std::filesystem` for file operations.
- Use `std::chrono` for time.
- Leverage latest C++23 features when available:
  - `std::expected` for error handling
  - `std::mdspan` for multidimensional array views
  - `std::ranges` and range adaptors
  - Modules when build system and toolchain support is mature

## Concurrency
- Use `std::thread`, `std::mutex`, `std::lock_guard` for thread safety.
- Prefer task-based parallelism over thread-based.
- Use `std::atomic` for atomic operations.
- Avoid data races with proper synchronization.
- Use thread-safe data structures when necessary.

## Testing
- Follow strict TDD: write tests before implementation.
- Use Catch2 for all unit and integration tests.
- Target at least 80% test coverage for all modules.
- All new features and bug fixes must include corresponding tests.
- Place tests in the `tests/` directory, mirroring the engine's module structure.
- Integrate tests with CTest and the build system.
- No code is considered complete until tests pass and coverage targets are met.
- Avoid obvious/unnecessary comments in tests, but provide Doxygen docs for test fixtures and non-obvious logic.


